MLX's CUDA quantized matmul has two kernels for a few rows of x: qmv, which
streams the whole weight matrix once per row, and the tiled qmm_sm80, which
reads it once but dequantizes into a tensor-core GEMM. On a GPU with little
compute per byte of memory bandwidth (GB10, sm_121) the tiled kernel costs
over twice a single row at any width, so a speculative-verify forward of 2..8
rows is slower than decoding its tokens one at a time.

This adds a rows variant of qmv: each warp dequantizes its row of w once and
takes its dot product with every row of x, in float32. Two or three rows cost
about what one does; each further row adds scalar compute only.

Off by default: MLX_CUDA_QMV_MAX_ROWS=<1..8> sets how many rows of one batch
take it (2D weights, no gather).

Drop this file and its step in build-mlx-linux.sh once lib/mlx-src includes
it.

